!id: sbybmrn4BP
!front: Simplify \( \sinh(\operatorname{arccosh}x) \) for \( x\ge 1 \), and justify the sign of the result.
!back:
Let:

- \( t=\operatorname{arccosh}x \), so \( \cosh t=x \) and \( t\ge 0 \).

To prove: \( \sinh t=\sqrt{x^2-1} \).

- The identity \( \cosh^2 t-\sinh^2 t=1 \) gives \( \sinh^2 t=x^2-1 \).
- Since \( t\ge 0 \), we have \( \sinh t\ge 0 \), so \( \sinh(\operatorname{arccosh}x)=\sqrt{x^2-1} \).
~~~

!id: ZgqaMdNJ7v
!front: Simplify \( \cosh(\operatorname{arcsinh}x) \) for \( x\in\mathbb{R} \), and justify the sign of the result.
!back:
Let:

- \( t=\operatorname{arcsinh}x \), so \( \sinh t=x \).

To prove: \( \cosh t=\sqrt{1+x^2} \).

- The identity \( \cosh^2 t-\sinh^2 t=1 \) gives \( \cosh^2 t=1+x^2 \).
- Since \( \cosh t>0 \) for every real \( t \), we have \( \cosh(\operatorname{arcsinh}x)=\sqrt{1+x^2} \).
~~~
